> > I think I now understand you problem.  I was testing my program with some
> > C++ library code.  I had problem running the analyzer with the source at
> > first, but this is what I did to get it running:
> > 
> > 1/ The C++ library comes with a make file.  In the makefile, it has a CC
> >    variable that holds the path for the compiler to use.  Assign the path
> >    of QCC to this CC variable.
> > 
> > 2/ The QCC script contains reference to the licence files.  This is how
> >    I modified it:
> > 
> > ======= Begin listing =====
> > #ident	"@(#)gen++:d.pkg/QCC	1.5"
> > export GENPP_LICENSE_FILE
> > GENPP_LICENSE_FILE=/home/s_fong/gen++/pkg/GENPP.dat
> > 
> > cfrontC=/home/s_fong/gen++/pkg/analyzer #EDIT: FULL PATH OF DESIRED ANALYZER
> > export cfrontC
> > CC -F "$@"
> > 
> > ======= End listing ======
> > 
> >    Basically, you replace GENPP_LICENSE_FILE by the absolute path of
> >    the GENPP.dat file, and replace cfrontC by the absolute path of the
> >    analyzer.
